From 0fa304b65c81eb59c58527e21b9ba6c3d34470f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Torra <n770galaxy@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 22:23:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 53/54] drm/gma500: size sgxfive render-context tiling buffers
 for a stated maximum

Every render context allocated the tail pointers and region headers of the
largest render target the device takes, 4096x4096: 1 MiB of zeroed lowmem
pages per context, most of which a context drawing to a small window or
to the 1024x600 panel never touches. The closed driver sizes this data per
surface when the surface is created, and powervr sizes each HWRT dataset
for its target.

CREATE_CONTEXT's former padding becomes max_width, followed by max_height
and new padding (the struct grows from 24 to 32 bytes). A render context
states the largest target its jobs will use, 1 to 4096 each way; zero, as
an older binary passes, is refused, and a transfer context must leave both
at zero. The kernel sizes the context's TA data for that target: its
layout is the largest of every target that fits, since the region headers
and tail pointers grow with each dimension and from two macrotiles to
four. The buffers are packed from the slot base, so a 4096x4096 maximum
keeps the previous fixed layout; the 2 MiB TA-data slots do not change. A
render job whose target is wider or taller than its context's maximum
fails with -EINVAL at submission. The debugfs triangle tests size their TA
data for their own 64x64 target.

A 64x64 context now pins 6 TA-data pages instead of 259 (4.5 MiB in all
instead of 5.5 MiB, the parameter buffer being most of it). KUnit checks
the sizes, including non-power-of-two targets and both sides of the
640x480 threshold, that each maximum covers every smaller target, the
addresses the builders use and the new argument and submission checks.

Assisted-by: LLM
